Z||000000||Haze_Performance||06-04-2000||11:46 AM||||Is there a 4-Speed Auto that will bolt right up the the 2.5L ?  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||06-04-2000||02:25 PM||||I know some Chevy Celebreties/ Pontiac 6000's had 4-speed autos and 2.5L engines. I'd give Bob a call at Twin Lakes Fiero. He was suppose to get some 4-speed autos for swaps at one time. (4's and 6's)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Haze_Performance||06-04-2000||02:28 PM||||Thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  ,Ill give him a call. ||||reg||7
Z||000003||terryk||06-04-2000||04:01 PM||||Yes, a 440 or 4T60 will bolt up to it. Just don't get a 4T60E. That will be stamped in the bottom of the tran pan.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfixit58||06-04-2000||07:50 PM||||Of course, there is still that nagging question about the axle lengths... same or different???<P>Roy||||reg||5
Z||000005||terryk||06-04-2000||08:07 PM||||125 and 440/4T60 use the same lengths. The 4T60E needs a short passenger side axle.||||reg||1
Z||000006||chris6878||06-05-2000||02:38 AM||||what cars would have these trannys?||||reg||3
Z||000007||Raydar||06-05-2000||11:52 AM||||TerryK,<BR>They use the same length?!?<BR>What is the difference? The splines?<BR>In every 125 to 4T60 swap that I've ever heard of, it required different axles.<BR>Am I missing something?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||06-05-2000||12:24 PM||||I was talking axle shaft lengths in a particular application. You still get to combine cv pieces to make it fit. When I lined up my 125C next to my 440 and 4T60E, the 125C and 440 are the same width. The 4T60E is slightly longer on the passenger side (thus needing a short axle shaft.)<P>You need the trans inner CV hoousings.<P>I should have said this earlier, I'd avoid the 440 and only go with a 4T60. At least it has the updates. I would never consider anything other than an extremely low miles 440. They were just too prone to failures.<P>Also, most 4 spds are geared pretty tall. The car will likely be slower with the 4 spd. If you want acceleration, stick with the 125C. Billions of them and they are durable transmissions.<P>You might want to contact CV's Unlimited and ask them to build you the axles assemblies. Explain that you want to go from a 440 to the Fiero 125C axles. They will understand what you mean. They built my Ciera axles.<P>IMHO<P>Terry<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfixit58||06-05-2000||01:38 PM||||Terry,<P>GREAT INFO! te 4-speed auto swap is looking more and more like a "bolt-in" project.<P>Now for some more questions...<BR>what about the speed-os, are they the same in each transmission? Do you have access to the different drive ratios for the 125C, 440, and the 4T60? <P>When I did the 700r4 swap into my buddy's 68 Camaro, I found out that both the 700r4 and the 200-4r had a LOWER first gear... meaning better acceleration. GM took advantage of the "extra" gear to spread the gears out a little bit and give the little motors (in big cars) a quicker feel. Seems that they would have done the same for the 440 and the 4T60 as well.<P>Comments?<P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||06-05-2000||05:18 PM||||Terry,<P>Thanks! Been looking for exactly that info for a while, now. <BR>Do you know what year the 4T60 became "electronic"? (4T60E?)||||reg||14
Z||000011||terryk||06-05-2000||08:43 PM||||I think the first 4T60E's were in 90, but I'm not completely positive. T60EHD's (heavy duty) in 91. I'll see if anything I have here says. <P>You can always tell a 4T60E because it has "4T60E" stamped in the pan (readable from the outside).<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000012||BroCar 87||06-05-2000||11:04 PM||||OK   just off the top of your heads,<P><BR>Its a 1991 Pontiac 6000 LE 3.1L (v-6)   Would that tranny be the same OR better than the 6000 2.5L??<P>And would it be able to bolt up to a 2.5 fiero engine like the 2.5 6000 does?<BR> <BR>I am just wondering if it would logically swap.||||reg||1
Z||000013||mrfixit58||06-06-2000||03:20 PM||||While doing some research on this topic, I stumbled across a very informative web page. For all thoses interested in the automatic OD swap, it has a LOT of good information.<BR> <A HREF="http://soli.inav.net/~pontiac/ratios.html" TARGET=_blank>http://soli.inav.net/~pontiac/ratios.html</A> <P>As an additional note, the first gear ratio on the 4T60 is numerically LARGER than the 125C (3T40). What this means is the motor has to make MORE revolutions to turn the wheel one complete revolution. What this means is a slightly better holeshot. Although the differences are not great (125C - 2.840; 4T60 - 2.921) at least your car won't be any slower.<P>I think this is great info and a auto w/OD swap would make a great "how-to" article. If anyone is planning on a swap, please let me know. I would be intersted in helping with the article AND, if your in the Tampa, FL area, be willing to donate some of my time to help twist wrenches!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Let's keep the info flowing!<P>Roy||||reg||2
Z||000014||mrfixit58||06-07-2000||11:03 AM||||Is everyone tired of this thread already? We still have some outstanding questions like: == Diminsions - width of 125C and 4T60 (for axles)?<BR>== Shift linkage - Need a kit? How to modify?<P>I think this is a very worthwhile conversion and would be a very useful upgrade to Automatic owners.<P>Anyone?<BR>Roy<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||BN Boomer||06-07-2000||01:36 PM||||I think a 4 speed auto would be a great swap. After reading this thread and that link in particular, I poked around the web and found a few of those F-7 versions of the TH-440-T4/4T60 that were beefed up for use in the Cadillac Allante starting in 1987. One of them is even not to far from my home. I'm still finishing up a current motor swap myself, but as soon as time permits I'm going to start investigating the possibility of eventually swapping one of these into my Fiero, possibly along with a 3.4L at some point in the next couple of years. I'd like to eyeball one in person, take a few measurements, check out how the shift linkage and park/neutral switch is mounted, and see what kind of bolt pattern the bell housing has.||||reg||1
